{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2291 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3f63bbe00ad544f18ba1cd6cedcfe8733d7e3532c511858941daf0b4778ae057" :edb/db-before-t 2290 :edb/db-after-t 2291 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535603 50 "2026-10-14T18:29:02.948Z" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186052384 1075 17592186522440 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186052384 1078 17592186522440 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054009 1078 17592186054014 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054540 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32$. Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2$, we set $b=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{16} (32) &= (\\log_{2} (32))/(\\log_{2} (16)) \\\\\n&= (\\log_{2} (2^{5}))/(\\log_{2} (2^{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186054540 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054541 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\log x ?$ \n\nI. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} x} {\\log_{2} 10}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} 10} {\\log_{2} x}$\n\nIII. $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right)$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054541 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\log x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} x} {\\log_{2} 10}$\n2. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} 10} {\\log_{2} x}$\n3. $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right)$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186054541 1074 "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=10$ to base $b=2$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log x &= \\log_{10} x \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\log_{2} x}{\\log_{2} 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186054541 1074 "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nThe change of base formula works with any new base. In this example, the choices are written using base $2,$ so we rewrite $\\log x$ in terms of $\\log_2.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡x & ={log}_{10}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{{log}_{2}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054543 1074 "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$, $X =7$, and $b=10$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} (7) &= (\\log_{10} (7))/(\\log_{10} (3)) \\\\\n&= (\\log (7))/(\\log (3)) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{0.84510}{0.47712} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.77\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186054543 1074 "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3,$ $X =7,$ and $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,10}{0.477\\,12} \\\\  & ≈1.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054612 1038 17592186522813 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054613 1038 17592186522719 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054740 1038 17592186522768 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054741 1038 17592186522701 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054742 1038 17592186522751 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054744 1038 17592186522741 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054744 1038 17592186522747 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054745 1038 17592186522725 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054745 1038 17592186522812 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186054890 1050 540.0 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055183 1070 17592186045487 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055183 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\log_{16} (64)$ equals {{field-1}}." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186055183 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${log}_{16}⁡(64)$ equals{{field-1}}." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055183 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=16$ and $X=64.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{16}⁡(64) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(64)}{{log}_{2}⁡(16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{6})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055462 1070 17592186045486 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055462 1071 "${log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055462 1071 "What is $\\log_{36} (\\sqrt[3]{6})$?" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186055462 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186055970 1064 "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$, but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a$, to another, $b$. Note that $X$, $a$, and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that\n\n$$\na \\ne 1\n$$\n\n$$\nb \\ne 1\n$$\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10$, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{4} 9 &= \\frac{\\log 9}{\\log 4} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{0.954242}{0.602059} \\\\\n&= 1.585\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186055970 1064 "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186059632 1038 17592186522694 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186059688 1038 17592186522706 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186059688 1038 17592186522795 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186059689 1038 17592186522788 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186059690 1038 17592186522800 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186061272 1078 17592186061275 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 17592186522731 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064376 1038 17592186522710 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064376 1038 17592186522715 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064376 1038 17592186522762 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064376 1038 17592186522772 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064376 1038 17592186522792 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064377 1038 17592186522702 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064377 1038 17592186522729 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064377 1038 17592186522743 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064377 1038 17592186522748 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064377 1038 17592186522775 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 17592186522703 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 17592186522716 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 17592186522722 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 17592186522794 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064507 1070 17592186045487 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064507 1071 "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ |" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186064507 1071 "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n = 30$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for $P(\\mid X^(―) \\mid > 0.2)$.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-0.7$ | $0.2389$ | $0.2358$ | $0.2327$ |\n| $-0.6$ | $0.2709$ | $0.2676$ | $0.2643$ |\n| $-0.5$ | $0.3050$ | $0.3015$ | $0.2981$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186064507 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=30,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 0.3+(-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =-0.6-0.2+0+0.8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4)-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.2+0.2+0+1.6)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{3}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,0.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =P(\\overset{―}{X}<-0.2)+P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.2) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}})+P(Z>\\frac{0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.63)+P(Z>0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.63)+1-P(Z<0.63) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63)=0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\,Φ(0.63)=1-Φ(-0.63).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =2Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5286.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186068701 1038 17592186522723 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186068750 1038 17592186522760 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186068750 1038 17592186522786 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186068750 1038 17592186522789 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186078383 1038 17592186522761 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186078408 1038 17592186522712 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083022 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ employees, so $n=20.$ We want to select $4$ of those employees to fill the roles of president, vice president, treasurer, and secretary, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to fill the roles is\n\n![](images/1a/1ae525b0e04502c3c1f4c8eb4ffd01419c48cbde67cbd721ad58300dc2f0ebb1.svg)" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083022 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ employees, so $n=20.$ We want to select $4$ of those employees to fill the roles of president, vice president, treasurer, and secretary, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to fill the roles is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{\\cancel{16!}} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =116\\,280.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186083112 1071 "Evaluate ![](images/77/777f12c125eef00b30f90e43ba94af90ac4b02b71c805da5b18e3306d03fba7d.svg)" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083112 1071 "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned})$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186083220 1038 17592186522745 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083221 1038 17592186522750 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083231 1038 17592186522802 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083311 1038 17592186522718 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083312 1038 17592186522815 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083359 1038 17592186522749 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083409 1050 420.0 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083411 1050 420.0 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186083458 1050 420.0 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186084450 1064 "In general, the number of unique combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\frac{{}_nP_r}{r!} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThis is pronounced \"en-see-arr.\" The \"C\" stands for \"combination.\" \n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books, taking $r=3$ books with us on the trip. Using the formula, the total number of combinations is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3!}{2!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times \\cancel{3!}}{2!\\cancel{3!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation for ${}_nC_r$ is $\\displaystyle {{n}\\choose{r}}.$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186084450 1064 "In general, the number of unique combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\frac{{}_nP_r}{r!} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThis is pronounced \"en-see-arr.\" The \"C\" stands for \"combination.\" \n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books, taking $r=3$ books with us on the trip. Using the formula, the total number of combinations is computed as\n![](images/e7/e73f73afd887785d06875907181fa38097aac62a65a81f5da464b674763ff863.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation for ${}_nC_r$ is $\\displaystyle {{n}\\choose{r}}.$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186084471 1064 "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\times 4\\times 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186084471 1064 "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n![](images/3d/3d53f73ea9e1a5d0966237c002f2d11075ee10d519bb6a2823b2ff0fea8c8e53.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186085195 1078 17592186085196 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1075 17592186086215 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186086214 1075 17592186086216 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186086214 1075 17592186086217 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186086214 1075 17592186086218 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186086214 1075 17592186086219 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186086214 1075 17592186522367 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1075 17592186522401 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1075 17592186522422 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1075 17592186522446 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1075 17592186522496 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186086214 1078 17592186522496 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186087491 1071 "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\ln(2)}$?" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186087491 1071 "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\ln(2)}?$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186087491 1074 "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n(4)/(\\ln (2)) \\mid \\approx \\frac{4}{0.69315}; \\approx 5.77076; \\approx 5.77\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186087491 1074 "W