\n\nas a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left(e^{-3} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(e^{-3}\\right) =-3\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186087497 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{e^3}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( \\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left( e^{-3} \\right).\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{-3}\\right) = - 3.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186087498 1071 "Solve the equation $\\, x^2-4x+5=0$." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186087498 1071 "Solve the equation $\\, x^2-4x+5=0.$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186087498 1074 "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1, b=-4$, and $c=5$. Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-4) ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± 2\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions,\n\n$$\nx=2+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=2-\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186087498 1074 "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1, b=-4,$ and $c=5.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we 