le such that $X\\sim U[2,8],$ find $\\textrm{E}[X].$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166583 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b]$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $b=8$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2+8}{2} =5\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186166583 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $b=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2+8}{2} =5.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166584 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X\\sim U[2,10]$, find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186166584 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X\\sim U[2,10],$ find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166584 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b]$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $b=10$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(10-2)^2}{12} = \\dfrac{64}{12} = \\dfrac{16}{3}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186166584 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $b=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(10-2)^2}{12} = \\dfrac{64}{12} = \\dfrac{16}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166632 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X\\sim U[-9,-3]$, find $\\textrm{SD}[X]$. Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186166632 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X\\sim U[-9,-3],$ find $\\textrm{SD}[X].$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166632 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b]$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-9$ and $b=-3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(-3-(-9))^2}{12} = \\dfrac{36}{12} = 3\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186166632 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-9$ and $b=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(-3-(-9))^2}{12} = \\dfrac{36}{12} = 3.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166743 1038 17592186522700 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166743 1038 17592186522790 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166743 1038 17592186522807 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166744 1038 17592186522738 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166744 1038 17592186522778 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166744 1038 17592186522784 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166745 1038 17592186522717 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166745 1038 17592186522733 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166745 1038 17592186522754 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166745 1038 17592186522758 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166745 1038 17592186522810 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 17592186522698 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 17592186522726 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 17592186522764 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 17592186522779 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 17592186522783 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 17592186522728 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 17592186522737 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 17592186522757 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 17592186522785 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166838 1038 17592186522724 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166849 1038 17592186522696 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166849 1038 17592186522752 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166849 1038 17592186522759 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166849 1038 17592186522799 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166850 1038 17592186522727 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166850 1038 17592186522740 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166850 1038 17592186522742 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166939 1038 17592186522787 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166940 1038 17592186522793 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186166961 1038 17592186522770 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167040 1050 420.0 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167267 1070 17592186045487 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167267 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$  {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167267 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 4λy = 0, y(0) = y^{′}(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1, λ \\ge 0$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem.\nThe eigenvalues are $λ_{n} =$ {{field-1}}, where $n ∈ N_{0}$.\n\nThe eigenfunctions are $y_{n}(x) =$ {{field-2}}, where $B_{n} ∈ R$." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186167267 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not a parameter of the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167324 1070 17592186045485 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167324 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X∼U[- 5, 4]$, find $SD[X]$. Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186167324 1071 "Given that $X$ is a random variable such that $X∼U[-5,4],$ find $\\text{SD}[X].$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186167324 1074 "If the random variable $X$ is distributed uniformly over the interval $[a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(4-(-5))^{2}}{12}=\\frac{81}{12}=\\frac{27}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}≈2.598.\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186168086 1064 "Recall that if the random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value and variance of $X$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{a + b}{2} \\\\\nVar[X] &= ((b - a)^{2})/(12)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result for $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is intuitive as it's simply the midpoint of the interval $[a,b]$.\n\nWe will derive both of these results at the end of the lesson." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186168086 1064 "Recall that if the random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/af/af2afd72c74a9d7ca810886490baf28610cb743cbe8c8595158cfc2af702b7fa.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value and variance of $X$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{a+b}{2} \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result for $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is intuitive as it's simply the midpoint of the interval $[a,b].$ \n\nWe will derive both of these results at the end of the lesson." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186168111 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe can compute the formula for $\\textrm E[X]$ using the usual definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\int_{a}^{b}xf(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{x}{b - a}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}\\right|_{a}^{b} \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b^{2} - a^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b + a) \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b + a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186168111 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/be/be9457e598579e35be37d8fcc86663f4229e6193f02b11c49242d63858406a3b.svg)\n\nWe can compute the formula for $\\textrm E[X]$ using the usual definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{x}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{2}-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186168112 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$, we use the relation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - (\\textrm{E}[X])^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{a}^{b}x^{2}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{x^{2}}{b - a}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}\\right|_{a}^{b} \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b^{3} - a^{3}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1)/(3(b - a)) \\cdot (b - a)(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac12(b+a)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n(\\textrm E[X])^2 = \\dfrac14(b^2+2ab+a^2)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - (E[X])^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) - \\frac{1}{4}(b^{2} + 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{12}(b^{2} + ab + a^{2}) - \\frac{3}{12}(b^{2} + 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(4b^{2} + 4ab + 4a^{2}) - \\frac{1}{12}(3b^{2} + 6ab + 3a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(b^{2} - 2ab + a^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(b - a)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186168112 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/57/579372cf7b7880117ff6e7b4e1f063b29e42f886b06e2277e2dcb1f1240ff11d.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we use the relation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - (\\textrm{E}[X])^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac12(b+a),$ we have\n\n$$\n(\\textrm E[X])^2 = \\dfrac14(b^2+2ab+a^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-(\\text{E}[X])^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{4}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{12}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{3}{12}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(4{b}^{2}+4ab+4{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{12}(3{b}^{2}+6ab+3{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({b}^{2}-2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(b-a)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172064 1038 17592186522765 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172064 1038 17592186522776 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172064 1038 17592186522801 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172065 1038 17592186522735 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172065 1038 17592186522769 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186172065 1038 17592186522773 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186175902 1038 17592186522705 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186176062 1038 17592186522709 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186179650 1038 17592186522697 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186183151 1078 17592186183153 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184171 1038 17592186522732 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184171 1038 17592186522756 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184172 1038 17592186522781 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184219 1038 17592186522771 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184219 1038 17592186522797 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184757 1070 17592186045487 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184757 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\n1. $rank(A) = 3$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $nullity(A) = 1$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186184757 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186184757 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186198397 1038 17592186522753 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186198397 1038 17592186522809 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186198398 1038 17592186522780 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186233738 1068 17592186522675 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314274 1075 17592186313946 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314274 1078 17592186312328 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314274 1078 17592186313946 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314430 1075 17592186313730 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314430 1078 17592186312166 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314430 1078 17592186313730 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314472 1075 17592186312788 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314472 1078 17592186312788 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314472 1078 17592186313814 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314743 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314743 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535603 17592186045417 false] [17592186314743 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" 13194139535603 17592186045417 true] [17592186314743 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary